Evidence-first rules:
- Treat the supplied master as evidence, not wording to paraphrase.
- Preserve protected structural fields exactly: employers, titles, dates,
  education, contact details, URLs, project names, and project technologies.
- Use target-role terminology only when it accurately maps to the evidence.
- Prefer concrete deliverables, technical mechanisms, constraints, and verified
  outcomes over generic claims or tool lists.
- Match language strength to ownership: contributed, implemented, co-developed,
  led, and owned are not interchangeable.
- Prefer the narrower defensible claim when evidence or an inference basis is
  ambiguous.
- Every returned item must be understandable, interview-defensible, and useful
  to the target application.
